<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Laws in Wales Acts 1535 and 1542</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1535">1535</a>–<a href="/years/1542">1542</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Laws in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> Acts were statutes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> that annexed Wales to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. They abolished the marcher lordships, created new shires such as Glamorgan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brecknockshire">Brecknockshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembrokeshire">Pembrokeshire</a>, gave Wales seats in Parliament and applied English law. They also required officials to use English, excluding Welsh speakers from public office.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/laws-in-wales-acts-1535-and-1542.jpg" alt="Laws in Wales Acts 1535 and 1542" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_Henry_VIII_of_England_(1509-1547).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It had expanded within the British Isles over centuries: Edward I conquered Wales, which was formally incorporated by the <b>Laws in Wales Acts</b> of 1535 and 1542, and English kings ruled the Lordship of Ireland from the 1170s and, from 1542, a Kingdom of Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Laws in Wales Act</b> of 1536 ended its special status, and Pembroke became the core of Pembrokeshire.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-pembroke">County Palatine of Pembroke</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Laws in Wales Act</b> of 1536 made Brecknock a shire, which survives as part of Powys.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-brecknock">Lordship of Brecknock</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Laws in Wales Act</b> of 1536 abolished the marcher lordships, and Glamorgan became a shire of the Kingdom of England, with Cardiff its county town.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-glamorgan">Lordship of Glamorgan</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Laws in Wales Act</b> of 1536 attached Gower to the new county of Glamorgan.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-gower">Lordship of Gower</a></li></ul></section>
